    73	      <section id="the-catch">
    74	        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
    75	          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
    76	          <ol>
    77	            <li><h3>The period is more than 30 days.</h3>The operative sentence says notice must arrive more than 30 days before implementation. “30 days” is a useful shorthand, but it drops the word that sets the boundary.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /></li>
    78	            <li><h3>The order follows the plans already put before the court.</h3>It does not say “any major physical changes” require notice. That phrase comes from the AP headline. The trigger in the reproduced order is a change to the scope of the project plans presented in the preservation case, with demolition named as an example.<Cite s="pbs-ap-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="any major physical changes" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" /></li>
    79	            <li><h3>Notice is not a final ruling on demolition.</h3>The reproduced sentence imposes an advance-notice requirement. It neither approves nor permanently bars demolition.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /></li>
    80	          </ol>
    81	        </aside>
    82	      </section>
    83	
    84	      <section id="where-this-sits">
    85	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The earlier record</p>
    86	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The court had relied on a promise of no full demolition or rebuilding</h2>
    87	        <p data-layer="narrative">In May, Cooper denied preliminary relief in the preservation case after considering sworn statements about the project. The opinion said the building's footprint and overall shape and size seemed unlikely to change permanently, and it described the anticipated open-space changes as relatively limited. The court said those findings were tentative and could change if the facts changed.<Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="footprint and overall shape and size seem unlikely to change in a permanent manner" /><Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="these relatively limited alterations" /><Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="tentative" /></p>
    88	        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 24, the Justice Department argued that without the administration's work and recognition, the Center would deteriorate until it was “required to be taken down.” The filing said some people had proposed replacing it with a large outdoor amphitheater overlooking the Potomac River. That was a conditional litigation position, not an approved demolition project.<Cite s="defendants-response-recognition-68-2026-08-24" passage="will be required to be taken down" /><Cite s="defendants-response-recognition-68-2026-08-24" passage="a large outdoor amphitheater overlooking the Potomac River" /></p>
    89	        <p data-layer="narrative">The parties' August 28 joint report made the relevant promise explicit: the defendants would inform the plaintiffs and the court if the board's plans reversed the position that the building would not be demolished and no new structure would be built.<Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="Defendants have pledged to inform Plaintiffs and the Court" /><Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="building will not be demolished and that no new structure will be constructed" /></p>
    90	        <p data-layer="narrative">Congress appropriated about {funding.appropriationLabel} for capital repair, restoration, the maintenance backlog and security structures, with the money available through {funding.availabilityDeadline}.<Cite s="public-law-119-21-kennedy-center" passage="$256,657,000, to remain" /><Cite s="public-law-119-21-kennedy-center" passage="available until September 30, 2029" /></p>
    91	        <p data-layer="narrative">The Atlantic reported that the new tax filing showed {financialFiling.totalRevenueLabel} in total revenue and a {financialFiling.badDebtExpenseLabel} bad-debt expense, that program-service revenue fell from {financialFiling.priorProgramRevenueLabel} to {financialFiling.currentProgramRevenueLabel}, and that the annual audit was unfinished.<Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="more than $516 million in total revenue" /><Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="$48 million “bad debt expense,”" /><Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="Program-service revenue" /><Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="the annual audit was not completed by the time of filing" /></p>
    92	        <p data-layer="narrative">The August minutes say that keeping the main building partly open would leave the appropriation covering only about half of the work, with a {funding.partialOperationShortfallLabel} shortfall after utilities. The minutes put the closure estimate at about {funding.closureEstimateLabel}.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="would cover only approximately half of the work" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="shortfall of $124M" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="approximately $285 million" /></p>
    93	        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>How the notice order arose</strong><span>The dated sequence in the court and board records.</span></figcaption><DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} /></figure>
    94	      </section>
    95	
    96	      <section id="who-feels-it">
    97	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What changes in practice</p>
    98	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure</h2>
    99	        <p data-layer="narrative">The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope change. It adds written notice to the earlier pledge to report a reversal of the position against full demolition or rebuilding.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases" /><Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="change in the Board’s plans that would constitute a reversal" /></p>
   100	        <p data-layer="narrative">The May opinion records Floca's estimate that between {affectedPeople.closureEstimateLow} and {affectedPeople.closureEstimateHigh} of roughly {affectedPeople.employeeEstimateTotal} employees would be affected by the closure. It also says several performance contracts would be affected, the Center had laid off at least {affectedPeople.layoffTranches} groups of staff including programming personnel, and {affectedPeople.remainingProgrammingStaff} programming staff remained.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="75 to 175 of roughly 300 employees at the Center would be affected" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="The closure will affect several existing performance contracts" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="laid off at least two tranches of staff members" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="just ten members of the programming staff remain" /></p>
   101	        <p data-layer="narrative">The opinion also identifies lost ticket and tourist revenue, trouble retaining audiences, the possible permanent loss of specialized arts professionals and long-term donor attrition among the downsides described in sworn testimony.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="lost ticket and tourist revenue; chronic challenges in audience retention" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="potentially permanent loss of highly-specialized arts professionals; and long-term attrition of donors" /></p>
   102	        <p data-layer="narrative">The August board plan keeps the REACH, the Center's expansion campus, open for educational and artistic programming, supports National Symphony Orchestra performances at off-site venues, and moves the Kennedy Center Honors and Mark Twain Prize off site while the main building is closed.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="expansion campus, known as the REACH" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="shall continue to present educational and artistic programming at the REACH" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="those performances offsite" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Kennedy Center Honors and the Mark Twain Prize for American Humor" /> The plan also moves the Kennedy memorial to the REACH and says it will remain open to the public.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="the Memorial to President John F. Kennedy be temporarily relocated to the REACH where it will remain open to the public" /></p>
   103	        <p data-layer="narrative">In a public statement, the {affectedPeople.preservationPlaintiffOrganizations} preservation plaintiffs said the court should consider a receiver or replacement leadership if the Center's leaders would not protect the building. They also named a limited receiver, independent monitor or other neutral officer. They framed those measures as steps for the court to consider.<Cite s="preservation-plaintiffs-statement-2026-09-17" passage="the court should consider appointing a receiver or replacing that leadership" /><Cite s="preservation-plaintiffs-statement-2026-09-17" passage="a receiver, limited receiver, independent monitor, or other neutral officer" /></p>
   104	        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants called the {closureDispute.closureDateLabel} safety closure separate from the renovation decision. Floca's staff email said the closure would let operations staff determine whether another severe safety incident was imminent and said it was not a decision to carry out the board's separate renovation-closure vote.<Cite s="floca-email-86-2-2026-09-16" passage="determine whether any other severe safety incident is imminent" /><Cite s="floca-email-86-2-2026-09-16" passage="this is not a decision to effectuate the Board's separate vote" /> Beatty's reply answered with a chronology: the building remained open for {closureDispute.daysOpenAfterIncident} days after the {closureDispute.incidentDateLabel} plaster fall, the Walter P Moore analysis dated from {closureDispute.assessmentMonthsLabel}, and the defendants identified no meaningfully new information between the incident and the closure. The closure came the day after the board vote.<Cite s="defendants-response-86-2026-09-17" passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="kept the building open for twelve days after September 4" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="identified no meaningfully new information" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="was completed in June and July of 2026" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="immediately followed the Board’s closure vote" /></p>
   105	        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling plaster after water damage. The same declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels and said one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.<Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="a roof terrace canopy assessment" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of one-inch-thick ceiling plaster on September 4" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="severe corrosion in 46 panels" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="One-third of all panels require immediate replacement" /> Cooper denied the requested hearing. Neither UPI's reproduced order text nor counsel's release reports a resolution of the parties' factual dispute.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="Beatty's request for a hearing was denied" /><Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="Judge Cooper additionally ordered discovery" /></p>
   106	        <p data-layer="narrative">Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion, said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit exterior pillars several times. That observation was sworn testimony from one witness, not a finding by the court.<Cite s="kennedy-bailey-declaration-84-1-2026-09-16" passage="Bridget Kennedy-Bailey" /><Cite s="kennedy-bailey-declaration-84-1-2026-09-16" passage="forklift run into and hit the pillars" /></p>
   107	        <p data-layer="narrative">The board's August minutes record {boardVote.votesCast} votes cast: {boardVote.inFavor} in favor and {boardVote.opposed} opposed. Another {boardVote.presentNotVoting} trustees were present but did not vote, and several were absent. The resolution directed closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorized legal steps to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Motion carried by roll call, 23 in favor, 3 opposed" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Present, not voting" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="seeking to dissolve the preliminary injunction" /></p>
   108	        <p data-layer="narrative">That vote came with acknowledged gaps. Beatty said she received {boardReview.slidesReceived} slides {boardReview.daysBeforeMeeting} days before the meeting, Whitehouse listed {boardReview.requestedAnalyses} baseline requirements, and Larsen said the board lacked the full {boardReview.missingComparisonYearsLabel} analysis. The defendants later said the full Delta report went to trustees on {boardReview.deltaDistributionDateLabel}.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="only two PowerPoint slides, received two days prior" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="summarizing his request in six points" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="characterized these as baseline requirements" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="did not have the full four-year analysis" /><Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="On August 31, the Center’s management distributed the finalized Delta Consulting" /></p>
   109	      </section>
