   126	      <section id="what-happened-next">
   127	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The next dated steps</p>
   128	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks</h2>
   129	        <p data-layer="narrative">The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.<Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Requests for Written and Document Discovery" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="All Discovery Closed" /></p>
   130	        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.<Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="required the Board to update the Court by September 23" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Motion to Vacate the Preliminary" /></p>
   131	        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.<Cite s="defendants-motion-vacate-88-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ MOTION TO DISSOLVE PRELIMINARY INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="defendants-summary-judgment-89-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ RENEWED MOTION FOR" /></p>
   132	        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca says management distributed a packet on {boardReview.managementPacketDateLabel} for discussion at the {boardReview.reconsiderationDateLabel} board meeting and attached it to his declaration as Exhibit B. The declaration dates the packet to the closure deliberations but does not reproduce its contents or identify the recognition proposal.<Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="On September 13, 2026, the Center distributed to trustees a packet of materials" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="attached to this declaration as Exhibit B" /></p>
   133	        <p data-layer="narrative">The defense filings agree that the board voted again to close the main building, but they quote the timing differently. The statement of facts says it would close to patrons immediately; the supporting memorandum says closure would follow dissolution of the preliminary injunction.<Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="shall be closed to patrons forthwith" /><Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="forthwith upon the dissolution of the preliminary injunction" /></p>
   134	        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants argued that phased closure would create a {funding.phasedClosureClaimLabel} funding shortfall, compared with less than {funding.fullClosureShortfallClaimLabel} for full closure. These were the defendants' assertions. Floca's declaration identifies an earlier JLL plan and Delta presentation as filed under seal. It says the finalized Delta report and certain appendices were attached as Exhibit A, while appendices with sensitive architectural drawings were excluded.<Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="a $300 million funding shortfall" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appears under seal on the docket" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="correct copy of that report and certain of its appendices is attached to this declaration as" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appendices that contain sensitive architectural drawings" /></p>
   135	        <p data-layer="narrative">Later on September 18, thousands joined the Hands Around the Kennedy Center protest, and some formed a human chain around the building. The organizer invited artists, patrons, staff members, union members and volunteers.<Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="Thousands of people joined outside" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="formed a human chain around the center" /><Cite s="action-network-hands-around-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="Worked at the Center as a staff member, union member, or volunteer" /> AP also reported that Duke Ellington students had lost access to Washington National Opera costumes and Millennium Stage performances.<Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="borrowed the beautiful costumes and the beautiful dresses from the Washington National Opera" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="performed on the center’s Millennium Stage" /></p>
   136	      </section>
   137	
   138	      <section id="unknowns">
   139	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
   140	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
   141	        <ul data-layer="fact">
   142	          <li data-absence>Whether the September 23 status report was filed, and whether the initial safety closure was extended. Searched: the admitted docket material and coverage through September 19; the report was not yet due.</li>
   143	          <li data-absence>Whether the defendants have developed a project-scope change that would trigger the notice order. The accessible public filings through September 19 do not identify an approved demolition plan. Floca's declaration identifies an earlier JLL report and Delta presentation filed under seal. It says the finalized Delta report and certain appendices were filed as Exhibit A, but that exhibit did not answer through the public docket route.</li>
   144	          <li data-absence>Which closure condition appears in the September 15 preliminary minutes. The two defense filings quote the resolution differently, and the preliminary minutes were not available through the public docket route.</li>
   145	          <li>Trump's intent remained unresolved on September 18. The Associated Press reported that he did not directly respond when asked whether he planned to demolish the theater.<Cite s="ap-trump-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="did not directly respond when a reporter asked whether he planned to demolish the theater" /></li>
   146	          <li data-absence>The court's minute order as a standalone, court-hosted document. Searched: the District Court docket, CourtListener and Justia; the docket routes were unavailable to the capture registry and the Justia mirror stopped before September 17. The page therefore labels the carriers of the quoted sentence.</li>
   147	        </ul>
   148	      </section>
