   370	
   371	
   372	Mr. Buchalter stated that the state of disrepair of the Center is significant and that the remedy will
   373	necessarily be invasive, and that the JLL report, the prior evidence, the site visits and the invasive testing
   374	currently underway all show rapidly accelerating deterioration. He further stated that these conditions
   375	“instantly challenging” to consider how the Center can continue to operate during the planned
   376	construction activities.
   377	
   378	Operate and partially renovate. The complexity of construction would be severe, requiring that patrons,
   379	workmen and equipment be safely channeled so that they never meet, producing an invasive visitor
   380	experience. We would have to expect having equipment on various decks, temporary entrances; a very
   381	invasive, frankly unpleaseant experience for someone visiting the Center. Costs more and takes longer.
   382	The $257 million appropriation would cover only approximately half of the work. Delta prioritized
   383	utilities — the entirety of the mechanical, electrical and plumbing systems, which are antiquated and in
   384	disrepair. In thirty-six years, Mr. Buchalter has only seen one electrical room that looks like the
   385	electrical room at the Center. Once the utilities work is completed (cost $225M) , there would be
   386	insufficient funds (shortfall of $124M) to complete the structural renovation and no remaining funds at
   387	all for venue improvement. Revenues would be expected to decline, a compression Delta believes is
   388	already visible in year-over-year revenue decline because of uncertainty and friction; would expect that
   389	to continue. The main building does not segment well, gravitating around a core rather than separable
   390	wings; on a four-year assumption, approximately a third of the building would have to be closed at any
   391	given time, reducing programming space by a third (to allow for equipment etc). That’s additional
   392	revenue degredation. The capital program would be underfunded by as much as half, while the Center
   393	runs a meaningful operating deficit, directing fundraising toward the deficit and capital gaps rather than
   394	toward an endowment.
   395	
   396	Close the main building. The $257 million appropriation covers most of the program. Escalating the
   397	2021 study to present-day dollars yields approximately $285 million; the resulting $30 million difference
   398	could be addressed through value engineering, modest fundraising, or scope adjustment. Executing over
   399	a certain two-year period rather than an uncertain four-year period produces a dramatically different
   400	exposure to cost escalation and a different level of safety. Delta believes operating costs can be
   401	curtailed. The Center can build excitement around the reopening. By leveraging the REACH and
   402	thinking about its mission, we can ensure it keeps the mandate during shutdown. Mr. Buchalter advised
   403	that he had been involved in renovation of Carnegie Libraries, so he understands the cultural mandate.
   404	The partial closure would be a much more virtuous use of fundraising dollars.
   405	
   406	Recommendation
   407	Mr. Buchalter stated Delta's recommendation to close the main building and renovate, as the most
   408	efficient, economical and safest way to maximize permanent capital improvements while maintaining
   409	mission continuity and the memorial experience.
